[size=4][b][u]Venworks Customizable HUD: Player Guide[/u][/b][/size]

This guide is for players who want to customize the look, placement, visibility, and colors of the Venworks Customizable HUD. You do not need to change HUD movies or write code; make changes in the supplied XML files.

For every supported XML element and attribute, use the
[url=https://github.com/monster-cookie/venworks-honkcore-ta-ui/blob/master/MarketingSites/NexusMods/LayoutConfigurationReference.bbcode]layout configuration reference[/url]. For a
complete palette contract and custom-theme workflow, use the
[url=https://github.com/monster-cookie/venworks-honkcore-ta-ui/blob/master/MarketingSites/NexusMods/PaletteConfigurationReference.bbcode]palette configuration reference[/url]. For reusable HUD sections, components, and display filters, use the [url=https://github.com/monster-cookie/venworks-honkcore-ta-ui/blob/master/MarketingSites/NexusMods/ComponentLibrary.bbcode]component library[/url].

[size=3][b][u]Before you edit[/u][/b][/size]

[list=1]
[*]Enable only one of the five Venworks Customizable HUD release variants. All variants install the same HUD and configuration paths, so they must never be enabled together.[/*]
[*]Fully exit Starfield.[/*]
[*]Back up the loose [font=Courier New]Interface\VenworksCUI\layout.xml[/font] from the normal package. If you use the fully loose package or an advanced override, back up the complete [font=Courier New]Interface\VenworksCUI[/font] directory.[/*]
[*]Use a plain-text or XML editor that preserves UTF-8 text and straight ASCII quotation marks.[/*]
[*]Keep a copy of every customized file outside the downloaded mod package.[/*]
[/list]

When replacing an experimental Bethesda Creation with an official release that owns the same ESM identity, uninstall the experimental entry first and verify that only one remains before installing the replacement; duplicate Creation entries can compete over the same plugin and BA2 files.

Mod-manager updates, reinstalls, purge/deploy operations, and file-conflict
changes can replace local edits. The normal Nexus package supplies only
[font=Courier New]layout.xml[/font] loose; its component fragments, palettes, and SVG assets remain in
the BA2. The safest long-term setup for advanced changes is a small personal
override mod containing only your changed [font=Courier New]Interface\VenworksCUI[/font] files and
loading after the selected theme variant. Alternatively, use the Nexus PC -
Fully Loose Files package by itself. If you edit a downloaded file directly,
keep a separate backup that an update cannot overwrite.

Do not edit [font=Courier New]hudmenu.gfx[/font], [font=Courier New]hudmenu_lrg.gfx[/font], or [font=Courier New]venworkscui.swf[/font] to make the XML changes in this guide. The base HUD movies load the packaged standalone configuration runtime automatically.

[size=3][b][u]Installed files[/u][/b][/size]

The release variants expose these runtime paths relative to Starfield's
[font=Courier New]Data\Interface[/font] directory:

[code]
VenworksCUI\
  layout.xml
  Assets\
    *.svg
  components\
    *.xml
  palettes\
    *.xml
[/code]

The important files are:

[list][*]Path — Purpose[/*][/list]
[list][*][font=Courier New]Interface\VenworksCUI\layout.xml[/font] — Selects the palette, defines shared meter styles, controls Bethesda HUD targets, places reusable fragments, and contains small root-level components.[/*][/list]
[list][*][font=Courier New]Interface\VenworksCUI\components\*.xml[/font] — Defines the contents of each reusable HUD section.[/*][/list]
[list][*][font=Courier New]Interface\VenworksCUI\palettes\*.xml[/font] — Defines semantic colors, typography, opacity, strokes, and the faction crest.[/*][/list]
[list][*][font=Courier New]Interface\VenworksCUI\Assets\*.svg[/font] — Contains supported local vector artwork used by the layout or palettes.[/*][/list]

Minimalist includes [font=Courier New]layout.xml[/font] and six component fragments, but intentionally omits the [font=Courier New]Assets[/font] and [font=Courier New]palettes[/font] directories. Its build resolves the shipped Starfield color roles to literals. The normal package exposes its layout loose; the fully loose package exposes the reduced XML tree and nine movies: byte-identical CWS normal/large HUD aliases at both [font=Courier New].gfx[/font] and [font=Courier New].swf[/font] paths, the native GFX/CWS HUD-message pairs, and the standalone CWS [font=Courier New]venworkscui.swf[/font] runtime.

The Nexus PC - Normal package exposes only [font=Courier New]layout.xml[/font] from this tree as a
loose file. The runtime resolves referenced component fragments and, when
configured, palettes and SVG assets from the package's BA2. Do not install the
normal and fully loose packages together.

When using a mod manager, open the active mod's own file directory rather than
assuming the deployed [font=Courier New]Data[/font] copy is the authoritative source. The exact
manager-specific staging location is intentionally not fixed by this project.

[size=3][b][u]How configuration loading works[/u][/b][/size]

At HUD startup, the base HUD bootstrap asynchronously loads [font=Courier New]Interface\venworkscui.swf[/font]. After the auxiliary bridge initializes, the runtime:

[list=1]
[*]loads [font=Courier New]layout.xml[/font];[/*]
[*]loads and places every declared component fragment;[/*]
[*]loads the selected palette;[/*]
[*]expands composites, templates, repeaters, and states;[/*]
[*]validates the resolved layout and all conditions and live-value bindings;[/*]
[*]preloads every referenced local SVG; and[/*]
[*]displays the complete custom layer only after those steps succeed.[/*]
[/list]

The process is atomic. One invalid file prevents the configurable layer from
partially rendering. A diagnostic panel identifies the failure category and,
when available, the phase, checkpoint, component type, and component ID.

Minimalist follows the same external configuration loading path and uses live data contexts. It omits only the four providers used exclusively by its removed equipment rail; all remaining conditions and live-value bindings stay active. Every variant uses the same nine-path movie inventory in its fully loose package and in each platform's Main archive: CWS normal/large HUD bytes under both [font=Courier New].gfx[/font] and [font=Courier New].swf[/font] aliases, native GFX/CWS HUD-message pairs, and [font=Courier New]venworkscui.swf[/font]. The base HUD and HUD-message paths are shared across all variants; Minimalist's standalone CUI movie is profile-specific.

There is no live reload command. After every XML or SVG change, fully exit and
restart Starfield. Merely closing the scanner or opening a menu is not a
guaranteed reload.

[size=3][b][u]Switch the active theme[/u][/b][/size]

Open [font=Courier New]Interface\VenworksCUI\layout.xml[/font] and change only the root [font=Courier New]palette[/font]
attribute:

[code]
<venworksCUI schemaVersion="1" runtimeVersion="1"
             designWidth="1920" designHeight="1080"
             safeLeft="64" safeTop="36"
             safeRight="64" safeBottom="36"
             palette="trackers-alliance.xml">
[/code]

The packaged choices are:

[list][*]Appearance — Palette filename[/*][/list]
[list][*]Venworks Customizable HUD - Venworks Theme — [font=Courier New]venworks.xml[/font][/*][/list]
[list][*]Venworks Customizable HUD - Trackers Alliance Theme — [font=Courier New]trackers-alliance.xml[/font][/*][/list]
[list][*]Venworks Customizable HUD - Freestar Collective Theme — [font=Courier New]freestar-collective.xml[/font][/*][/list]
[list][*]Venworks Customizable HUD - Crimson Fleet Theme — [font=Courier New]crimson-fleet.xml[/font][/*][/list]
[list][*]Neutral Starfield option — [font=Courier New]starfield.xml[/font][/*][/list]

The filename must name one XML file directly inside
[font=Courier New]Interface\VenworksCUI\palettes[/font]. Subdirectories, path traversal, URLs, query
strings, and fragments are rejected.

Every themed release variant includes all five palette XML files. The themed
variant only chooses the initial default. All five current palette logo
references resolve to SVG files included with the themed HUD. Minimalist does
not ship an external palette or SVG asset, and its root layout has no palette
selector, although its movie retains the same palette and SVG runtime.

[size=3][b][u]Move a complete HUD section[/u][/b][/size]

Reusable HUD sections are placed in the [font=Courier New]<includes>[/font] block of [font=Courier New]layout.xml[/font].
Each [font=Courier New]<include>[/font] has an [font=Courier New]x[/font], [font=Courier New]y[/font], and optional [font=Courier New]anchor[/font]:

[code]
<include id="equipment-rail" src="equipment-rail.xml"
         x="64" y="36" anchor="top-right"
         visible="true" visibleWhen="always" z="102" />
[/code]

For anchored sections, [font=Courier New]x[/font] and [font=Courier New]y[/font] are signed offsets from the selected anchor
inside the root safe area. Increasing [font=Courier New]x[/font] moves the section right; increasing
[font=Courier New]y[/font] moves it down. Negative values move left or up.

The nine supported anchors are:

[code]
top-left       top-center       top-right
center-left    center           center-right
bottom-left    bottom-center    bottom-right
[/code]

The production fragments are:

[list][*]Include ID — File — HUD section[/*][/list]
[list][*][font=Courier New]faction-display[/font] — [font=Courier New]faction-display.xml[/font] — Palette-selected faction crest.[/*][/list]
[list][*][font=Courier New]contact-radar[/font] — [font=Courier New]contact-radar.xml[/font] — Bounded acquired-contact radar.[/*][/list]
[list][*][font=Courier New]quest-tracker[/font] — [font=Courier New]quest-tracker.xml[/font] — Persistent tracked objective.[/*][/list]
[list][*][font=Courier New]environmental-hazard-scanner[/font] — [font=Courier New]environmental-hazard-scanner.xml[/font] — Location, planet, local solar-transition countdown, protection, and exposure status.[/*][/list]
[list][*][font=Courier New]player-status-scanner[/font] — [font=Courier New]player-status-scanner.xml[/font] — Player statistics and meters.[/*][/list]
[list][*][font=Courier New]equipment-rail[/font] — [font=Courier New]equipment-rail.xml[/font] — Favorites, weapon, explosive, and power information.[/*][/list]
[list][*][font=Courier New]helmet-awareness[/font] — [font=Courier New]helmet-awareness.xml[/font] — Compass, threat state, and status effects.[/*][/list]
[list][*][font=Courier New]scanner-overlay[/font] — [font=Courier New]scanner-overlay.xml[/font] — Scanner-only pulse and forward contacts.[/*][/list]

Change one axis at a time in small increments, restart the game, and check
normal, aiming, scanner, and vehicle states. A placement that looks correct in
one state can overlap Bethesda-owned content in another.

[size=3][b][u]Hide a complete HUD section[/u][/b][/size]

Set the include's [font=Courier New]visible[/font] attribute to [font=Courier New]false[/font]:

[code]
<include id="faction-display" src="faction-display.xml"
         x="-64" y="-36" anchor="top-left"
         visible="false" visibleWhen="always" z="103" />
[/code]

Keep the include in the file. Removing it also works structurally, but toggling
[font=Courier New]visible[/font] makes the customization easier to maintain and reverse.

[font=Courier New]visible="false"[/font] always hides the section. [font=Courier New]visibleWhen[/font] cannot override a
static false value.

[size=3][b][u]Show a section only in a specific state[/u][/b][/size]

Use [font=Courier New]visibleWhen[/font] with an allowlisted condition:

[code]
<include id="scanner-overlay" src="scanner-overlay.xml"
         x="0" y="0" anchor="center"
         visible="true" visibleWhen="inScanner" z="109" />
[/code]

Conditions are case-insensitive and ignore underscores. They support [font=Courier New]AND[/font],
[font=Courier New]OR[/font], [font=Courier New]NOT[/font], parentheses, and the numeric comparison operators [font=Courier New]=[/font], [font=Courier New]!=[/font],
[font=Courier New]<>[/font], [font=Courier New]<[/font], [font=Courier New]<=[/font], [font=Courier New]>[/font], and [font=Courier New]>=[/font].

Examples:

[code]
visibleWhen="inScanner"
visibleWhen="inCombat AND NOT inScanner"
visibleWhen="firstPerson AND hudVisible"
visibleWhen="weaponAiming OR inScanner"
visibleWhen="hudOpacityPercentage >= 50"
[/code]

Unknown live state evaluates as unknown and therefore remains hidden. Use the
[url=https://github.com/monster-cookie/venworks-honkcore-ta-ui/blob/master/MarketingSites/NexusMods/LayoutConfigurationReference.bbcode]condition reference[/url]
for the complete allowlist and expression limits.

[size=3][b][u]Adjust the root safe area[/u][/b][/size]

The root layout always uses a 1920-by-1080 design coordinate system. The four
safe-area values inset the area used by root anchors from Starfield's visible
rectangle:

[code]
safeLeft="64" safeTop="36" safeRight="64" safeBottom="36"
[/code]

Larger values move anchored content farther inward from that edge. Values must
be non-negative. [font=Courier New]safeLeft + safeRight[/font] cannot exceed 1920, and
[font=Courier New]safeTop + safeBottom[/font] cannot exceed 1080. The insets must leave a nonempty
visible area.

Safe-area changes affect anchored root components and included fragments. They
do not apply nonuniform screen scaling and should be tested at the actual aspect
ratio where they will be used.

[size=3][b][u]Move, resize, scale, or fade an individual component[/u][/b][/size]

Open the owning file under [font=Courier New]Interface\VenworksCUI\components[/font]. Most rendered
components share these attributes:

[code]
<group id="unique.name"
       x="0" y="0" width="320" height="80"
       opacity="1" visible="true" visibleWhen="always"
       rotation="0" scaleX="1" scaleY="1" z="1"
       anchor="top-left">
  <!-- Child components. -->
</group>
[/code]

[list]
[*][font=Courier New]x[/font] and [font=Courier New]y[/font] place the component relative to its parent when no anchor is present, or offset it from its anchor when an anchor is present.[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]width[/font] and [font=Courier New]height[/font] define design-space bounds. Most visible components require positive values.[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]opacity[/font] ranges from [font=Courier New]0[/font] through [font=Courier New]1[/font].[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]rotation[/font] is expressed in degrees.[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]scaleX[/font] and [font=Courier New]scaleY[/font] are finite multipliers. Use equal values to preserve proportions.[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]z[/font] is an integer used to order siblings; larger values draw above smaller values.[/*]
[/list]
[list]
[*][font=Courier New]visible[/font] and [font=Courier New]visibleWhen[/font] combine: both must permit display.[/*]
[/list]

To scale a complete imported fragment, change [font=Courier New]scaleX[/font] and [font=Courier New]scaleY[/font] on the
fragment's one root [font=Courier New]<group>[/font]. Include declarations do not have scale
attributes. Scaling does not automatically reflow surrounding sections, so
recheck overlaps and anchor offsets.

Keep every [font=Courier New]id[/font] unique within its document. IDs must start with a letter, may
contain letters, numbers, dots, underscores, and hyphens, and may be no longer
than 64 characters.

[size=3][b][u]Control approved Bethesda HUD sections[/u][/b][/size]

The optional [font=Courier New]<vanillaVisibility>[/font] block in [font=Courier New]layout.xml[/font] can conditionally show,
hide, or reposition only six whole Bethesda HUD targets:

[list][*]Target ID — Bethesda display[/*][/list]
[list][*][font=Courier New]topCenter[/font] — Top-center HUD group.[/*][/list]
[list][*][font=Courier New]bottomLeft[/font] — Watch/environment HUD group.[/*][/list]
[list][*][font=Courier New]rightMeters[/font] — Right-side meters.[/*][/list]
[list][*][font=Courier New]socialCommandIcons[/font] — Social command icons.[/*][/list]
[list][*][font=Courier New]floatingQuestMarkers[/font] — Floating quest markers.[/*][/list]
[list][*][font=Courier New]crewBuffWidget[/font] — Crew buff widget.[/*][/list]

Target names are case-insensitive and ignore underscores. The production layout
suppresses the Bethesda Watch/environment group and right-side meters with real
display visibility:

[code]
<vanillaVisibility>
  <target id="bottomLeft" visibleWhen="never" />
  <target id="rightMeters" visibleWhen="never" />
</vanillaVisibility>
[/code]

Do not use an offset to move an unwanted target outside the design area. That
is not a visibility contract and can fail under UI scaling or aspect-ratio
changes.

Relative placement requires [font=Courier New]offsetX[/font] and [font=Courier New]offsetY[/font] together and adds them to
Bethesda's original position. Absolute placement requires [font=Courier New]x[/font], [font=Courier New]y[/font], and
[font=Courier New]anchor[/font] together. Do not combine relative and absolute placement on one
target.

The adapter still composes your condition with Bethesda's own HUD-mode
visibility and HUD opacity. It does not force an engine-hidden element to
display.

Do not attempt to name a timeline clip or arbitrary Bethesda class. Only the
six allowlisted target IDs are accepted.

[size=3][b][u]Make basic color changes[/u][/b][/size]

Do not replace palette references throughout the layout for ordinary theme
changes. Instead:

[list=1]
[*]Copy a complete packaged palette XML file.[/*]
[*]Give the copy a safe filename such as [font=Courier New]my-hud.xml[/font].[/*]
[*]Keep it directly under [font=Courier New]Interface\VenworksCUI\palettes[/font].[/*]
[*]Change the desired [font=Courier New]value="#RRGGBB"[/font] entries.[/*]
[*]Point [font=Courier New]layout.xml[/font] at [font=Courier New]palette="my-hud.xml"[/font].[/*]
[/list]

For example:

[code]
<color role="accent.primary" value="#19C8FF" />
<color role="panel.background" value="#061722" />
<color role="marker.hostile" value="#FF3C54" />
[/code]

Always begin with a complete shipped palette. The runtime requires every core
semantic role even if the current layout does not visibly use it. Colors must
use six-digit [font=Courier New]#RRGGBB[/font]; shorthand and alpha-channel forms are rejected.

See the [url=https://github.com/monster-cookie/venworks-honkcore-ta-ui/blob/master/MarketingSites/NexusMods/PaletteConfigurationReference.bbcode]palette configuration reference[/url]
before changing typography, strokes, opacities, or faction artwork.

[size=3][b][u]Troubleshooting[/u][/b][/size]

[size=2][b][u]The custom HUD is replaced by an error panel[/u][/b][/size]

Read the title and first detail line. Common categories are:

[list][*]Diagnostic — Check[/*][/list]
[list][*][font=Courier New]CUI LAYOUT MISSING[/font] — Confirm [font=Courier New]Interface\VenworksCUI\layout.xml[/font] exists in the active deployment.[/*][/list]
[list][*][font=Courier New]CUI LAYOUT MALFORMED[/font] — Check XML quoting, closing tags, and entity escaping.[/*][/list]
[list][*][font=Courier New]CUI LAYOUT INVALID[/font] — Check the named element, attribute, ID, limit, reference, condition, or source.[/*][/list]
[list][*][font=Courier New]CUI COMPONENT MISSING[/font] — Confirm the named fragment exists directly under [font=Courier New]components[/font].[/*][/list]
[list][*][font=Courier New]CUI PALETTE MISSING[/font] — Confirm the root [font=Courier New]palette[/font] filename exists directly under [font=Courier New]palettes[/font].[/*][/list]
[list][*][font=Courier New]CUI PALETTE INVALID[/font] — Restore all required roles and valid values from a packaged palette.[/*][/list]
[list][*][font=Courier New]CUI ASSET LOAD ERROR[/font] — Confirm the named local SVG exists under [font=Courier New]Assets[/font] and uses the supported subset.[/*][/list]
[list][*][font=Courier New]CUI LAYOUT SECURITY ERROR[/font] — Scaleform denied access to [font=Courier New]layout.xml[/font]; check deployment and file access. Unsafe include names are reported as layout-invalid errors instead.[/*][/list]
[list][*][font=Courier New]CUI PALETTE SECURITY ERROR[/font] — Remove traversal, subdirectories, URLs, schemes, query strings, or fragments from the palette selection/asset, or check file access.[/*][/list]

The diagnostic may also show [font=Courier New]PHASE[/font], [font=Courier New]CHECKPOINT[/font], and [font=Courier New]COMPONENT[/font]. Use the
component ID to search [font=Courier New]layout.xml[/font] and every file under [font=Courier New]components[/font].

[size=2][b][u]The theme changed but the old colors remain[/u][/b][/size]

[list]
[*]Fully exit and restart Starfield.[/*]
[*]Confirm the deployed [font=Courier New]layout.xml[/font], not only a mod-manager staging copy, names the expected palette.[/*]
[*]Check which mod wins [font=Courier New]Interface\VenworksCUI\layout.xml[/font] conflicts.[/*]
[*]Check for a personal override that still contains an older layout or palette.[/*]
[/list]

[size=2][b][u]A moved section does not appear[/u][/b][/size]

[list]
[*]Temporarily set [font=Courier New]visible="true"[/font] and [font=Courier New]visibleWhen="always"[/font].[/*]
[*]Return [font=Courier New]opacity[/font], [font=Courier New]scaleX[/font], and [font=Courier New]scaleY[/font] to [font=Courier New]1[/font].[/*]
[*]Return the include to its shipped anchor and offsets.[/*]
[*]Check whether the section was moved outside the 1920-by-1080 design area.[/*]
[*]Check whether the error panel reports a different invalid component that prevented the entire custom layer from loading.[/*]
[/list]

[size=2][b][u]Restore the shipped configuration[/u][/b][/size]

Disable or remove your personal override, then reinstall or redeploy the chosen
theme variant. If you edited the active package directly, replace the complete
[font=Courier New]Interface\VenworksCUI[/font] directory from your backup or a clean copy of the same
release. Do not combine configuration files from different release versions
unless you have checked their [font=Courier New]schemaVersion[/font] and [font=Courier New]runtimeVersion[/font] contracts.

[size=3][b][u]Safe customization checklist[/u][/b][/size]

Before considering a customization finished:

[list]
[*]restart Starfield after the final change;[/*]
[*]test normal, aiming, scanner, combat, sneaking, vehicle, and low-health states;[/*]
[*]test the normal HUD and the large-HUD configuration you actually use;[/*]
[*]test at the target aspect ratio and HUD opacity;[/*]
[*]verify Bethesda-owned reticles, crosshairs, enemy health, and prompts still follow their original lifecycle;[/*]
[/list]
[list]
[*]keep the diagnostic panel absent during startup and transitions; and[/*]
[*]preserve a clean copy of every customized file outside the managed mod.[/*]
[/list]
